'''transparent''' (trȧns-pãr′ent), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< F. ''transparent'' = Pr. ''transparent'' = Sp. ''transparente'', ''trasparente'' = Pg. ''transparente'' = It. ''trasparente'', < ML. ''transparere'', shine through: see ''transpare''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' 1. Having the property of transmitting rays of light so that bodies situated beyond or behind can be distinctly seen; transmitting light-waves radiated from some source, without absorption or scattering; pervious to light; diaphanous; pellucid: as, ''transparent'' glass; a ''transparent'' diamond: opposed to ''opaque'', and distinguished from ''translucent''.

{{smaller block|Nor shines the silver moon one half so bright
Through the ''transparent'' bosom of the deep.
{{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., iv. 3. 31.}}}}

2. Admitting the passage of light through interstices.

{{smaller block|And Heaven did this ''transparent'' veil provide,
Because she had no guilty thoughts to hide.
{{right|''Dryden'', Epitaph on Monument of a Lady at Bath.}}}}

3. Figuratively, easily seen through or understood; easily intelligible.

{{smaller block|He was to exhibit the specious qualities of the tyrant in a light which might render them ''transparent'', and enable us at once to perceive the covering and the vices which it concealed. {{right|''Macaulay'', History.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Transparent'' discourse to a popular audience will be largely Saxon in its vocabulary.
{{right|''A. Phelps'', English Style, p. 150.}}}}

4. Bright; shining; clear.
